WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
A media team wants a night shot of a restaurant opening, with people on the sidewalk and cars moving through the foreground.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.720
The client has property permission and the drone is registered.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.060
The pilot has a Remote Pilot Certificate.

4
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:22.000
None of that automatically authorizes flight over people, sustained flight over vehicles, operation in controlled airspace, or a departure from Part 107 limits.

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:28.900
Special operations require separating each requirement and confirming the exact conditions that apply.

6
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:44.700
Routine Part 107 night operations are allowed when the remote pilot in command has completed the applicable updated initial knowledge test or online recurrent training and the small unmanned aircraft has lighted anti-collision lighting visible for at least 3 statute miles with a flash rate sufficient to avoid collision.

7
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.160
The pilot may reduce the intensity, but not extinguish the lights, when safety requires it.

8
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.700
During civil twilight, the lighting requirement also applies.

9
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.200
Night skill is more than turning on a strobe.

10
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:05.420
Reduced depth perception, glare, dark backgrounds, illuminated obstacles, and fatigue all affect visual line of sight and emergency planning.

11
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.500
Operations over people are condition-based.

12
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:21.400
A small unmanned aircraft may fly over people directly participating in the operation, or over people under a covered structure or inside a stationary vehicle that provides reasonable protection.

13
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:27.420
Otherwise, the operation must satisfy one of the Part 107 operational categories.

14
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:40.000
Category 1 uses an aircraft weighing 0.55 pounds or less throughout the operation, including everything attached, and no exposed rotating parts that would lacerate human skin.

15
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:50.020
Categories 2 and 3 require eligible aircraft, accepted declarations of compliance, labels, operating instructions, and performance-based injury limits.

16
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:56.280
Category 3 has additional restrictions and does not allow operations over open-air assemblies.

17
00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:03.020
Category 4 requires an airworthiness certificate and compliance with operating limitations.

18
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.380
Moving vehicles add another layer.

19
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:19.000
For Category 1, 2, or 3 operations over people inside moving vehicles, the aircraft must remain within or over a closed or restricted-access site with occupants on notice, or must not maintain sustained flight over moving vehicles.

20
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.740
Category 4 depends on its airworthiness certificate and operating limitations.

21
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.640
A one-time incidental transit is different from hovering or tracking a lane of traffic.

22
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:38.820
If the restaurant shot requires repeated passes above cars on a public street, the operation likely needs redesign, different control of the site, or a different authorization strategy.

23
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.500
Remote ID is often misunderstood.

24
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:49.280
It is an identification and accountability requirement, not airspace authorization and not permission to fly over people.

25
00:02:49.380 --> 00:03:01.060
A pilot generally complies by using a standard Remote ID aircraft, attaching a Remote ID broadcast module, or operating within an FAA-Recognized Identification Area when permitted.

26
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.000
A broadcast module does not remove visual line-of-sight requirements.

27
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:17.820
Drone registration is also separate: Part 107 registration is per drone, costs a fee, and is valid for three years under current FAA guidance.

28
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:24.320
The registration marking and Remote ID serial information support accountability; they do not make a mission safe.

29
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.400
Waivers are similarly specific.

30
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:34.140
The FAA may issue a certificate of waiver only when the proposed operation can be conducted safely under the waiver's terms.

31
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:41.680
The pilot may deviate only from the regulation and only to the extent specified, while complying with all conditions and limitations.

32
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.100
LAANC approval is not a waiver.

33
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.080
A waiver is not unrestricted permission.

34
00:03:47.180 --> 00:04:07.260
The practical takeaway for the night restaurant mission is to build a compliance matrix: certificate, recency, aircraft registration, Remote ID, night lighting, airspace authorization, people-overflight eligibility, moving-vehicle eligibility, property access, local restrictions, NOTAMs, TFRs, and site controls.

35
00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:11.040
If one required item is missing, redesign or decline.
